  "markdown_report": "**Synthesis.** Three starting insights guide this early-stage proposal. (1) Existing margins measure training discrimination, not immediate-purpose relevance or generalization. Missing measurements must remain null. (2) The backbone implementation is unavailable, so readout, causality, normalization, and exported scoring require verification. (3) Feedback motivates investigating recent-history dependence, but neither recent interactions nor deletion-induced accuracy drops establish intent or excessive recency bias.\n\nThree literature insights refine the approach. (1) NextItNet’s causal convolutions constrain accessible history [1]; actual dependency sets must govern temporal comparisons. (2) Sampled rankings can reverse model comparisons [2]; diagnostic rankings remain candidate-conditional and separate from benchmark selection. (3) Recency can help or hurt [3], while missing-input sensitivity also reflects robustness [4]; endpoint, content, and ordering effects require separate controls.\n\n**Directions and framework.** Preserve three layers: implementation integrity → measured behavioral sensitivity → hypothesis generation. Integrity covers padding, causal normalization, dependency sets, and predictor parity. Sensitivity covers matched edits, fixed candidates, and paired ranking changes. Hypotheses connect measurements with qualitative feedback without identifying causal effects on preferences. Purpose, duplication, and compatibility remain unknown without supporting public titles/descriptions; unavailable ratings, categories, reviews, and simulator traits never enter the framework.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality / future potential / code difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Contract-checked sensitivity adapter: 4/7/5.** Originality: integrates family-specific checks with behavioral probes, although components are established. Potential: supports subsequent experiments, although better diagnostics need not improve HR@10. Difficulty: needs no retraining, although dependency analysis, export parity, and matched controls exceed simple inference hooks [1,3,4].\n- **Matched recent-versus-older deletion probe: 5/7/6.** Originality: eligibility and structural matching sharpen attribution, although deletion tests are established. Potential: investigates stale-history dependence, although compaction changes distances and reachable content. Difficulty: avoids retraining, although valid matching and transition checks require substantial bookkeeping [1,4,5].\n- **Candidate-size stability audit: 3/6/4.** Originality: applies established sampling critiques with limited novelty. Potential: reveals ranking fragility, although it cannot replace benchmark selection. Difficulty: reuses logits, although full-catalog scoring and dependence-aware uncertainty add cost [2,6].\n\n**Selected idea: contract-checked sensitivity adapter.** At 0% progress, establish measurement validity before changing architecture. Preserve one independently trained NextItNet checkpoint per category, all host interfaces, and the unchanged pinned macro validation HR@10 objective.\n\nFirst verify heavily left-padded readout, future-token isolation at intermediate positions, exact architectural dependencies, and channel-only normalization [1,7,8]. Compute dependency offsets through every convolution and residual branch; the maximum receptive-field width alone can conceal unreachable offsets. Compare actual exported predictor scores and rankings with ModelView for identical singleton/batched inputs and category routing. Separate synthetic architecture tests from trained-model sensitivity: invariance is required outside dependencies, whereas zero sensitivity inside them need not indicate a defect.\n\nNext freeze chronological training prefixes and one candidate set per category/user/prefix: target once plus 99 distinct negatives excluding padding, target, and original-prefix items. Retain repeated targets, report repeat/novel strata, and explicitly report insufficient catalogs. Reuse candidates across edits; resolve ties using a seeded item ordering independent of target identity [2]. Never include the target event or subsequent events in its input.\n\nCompare unchanged histories, prefix shuffles retaining the endpoint and item multiset, endpoint-only replacements, and matched recent/older deletions. Match accessible positions, edit count, resulting length, endpoint status, and contiguous/scattered structure. Record changed distances, newly reachable items, and newly joined transitions; stratify by training-only frequency, transition support, timestamp gaps, and textual coherence where available [4,5]. Keep position-preserving real-item replacements separate; internal padding and implausible edits are stress tests. Report unavailable matches without shrinking edits silently.\n\nAggregate prefixes within users, users within categories, then four category means equally. Report paired target-rank changes, margin changes, hit@10 flips, eligibility counts, and missingness. Use seed 42 and 10,000 stratified user-cluster bootstrap replicates with paired conditions retained [6]. Intervals condition on fixed models, prefixes, and candidates. Optimized training prefixes provide in-sample sensitivity, not generalization; repeated tuning against validation can still overfit. Preserve metric definitions, null empty results, bounded probe cost, and measured-evidence interpreter prompts.\n\nReferences: [1] [NextItNet](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163). [2] [Krichene–Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/). [3] [Measuring Recency Bias](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722). [4] [Missing-input study](https://iris.uniroma1.it/bitstream/11573/1740238/1/Siciliano_Robust-Training_2024.pdf). [5] [CETD](https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/13/20/11176). [6] [Cluster bootstrap](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5965657/). [7] [NextItNet reference implementation](https://recommenders-team.github.io/recommenders/_modules/recommenders/models/deeprec/models/sequential/nextitnet.html). [8] [BatchNorm documentation](https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.modules.batchnorm.BatchNorm1d.html).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a contract-checked diagnostic adapter for the single trained NextItNet model in each category. Verify deployed scoring and causal dependencies before measuring endpoint, ordering, and recent-versus-older history sensitivity with fixed candidates and matched controls.",
    "motivation": "Existing training-position margins neither verify deployment correctness nor establish current-purpose relevance. Qualitative feedback motivates investigating stale-history dependence, but missing-input effects, architectural reachability, and candidate sampling can produce misleading conclusions without controls.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), returning metrics and metric_definitions. Access the actual network through view.model; preserve item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data without assuming unavailable metadata exists. Adapt tensor/NumPy conversions and candidate dimensions to the supplied ModelView rather than assuming reduced features are supported. Read each sequence at its maximum nonzero index; skip empty histories and positions with zero targets. For repeated targets, exclude the target event and later events from the input, while allowing earlier occurrences of that item. Inspect the supplied architecture before deriving dependency sets: compose convolution offsets and union residual paths; width is 1 + sum((kernel_size-1)*dilation) along the longest sequential path, including every convolution. Audit normalization over time: BatchNorm1d on B×C×L can leak future information during training, and temporal InstanceNorm, GroupNorm, or LayerNorm can do likewise; channel-only LayerNorm on B×L×C preserves positional independence. Test future-token invariance at multiple intermediate positions in evaluation and deterministic training modes using disposable copies, preserving live checkpoint buffers and random states. Test outside-dependency invariance, and exercise reachable boundaries with controlled weights in synthetic copies; trained-model insensitivity inside the dependency set is permissible. Compare the actual exported predictor against ModelView using identical histories, candidate IDs, truncation, padding, and routing, including singleton/batched calls; record score tolerances and rank agreement. Use predeclared eligible prefixes, edit counts, matching criteria, prefix caps, and bounded batch limits recorded in an artifact manifest beneath the trial directory. A normal training loader may lack stable user/prefix identifiers: build an explicit diagnostic index from public training histories and map loader rows to it only when identity is unambiguous; otherwise report user-cluster analyses unavailable. Freeze 99 unique negatives per category/user/prefix using stable, independently derived seeds, excluding padding, target, and items observed in the original prefix. Retain the target exactly once and keep candidates unchanged after edits; report cases lacking 99 eligible negatives. Report repeated-target and novel-target strata separately; an unseen-target-only analysis must report its coverage. Break ties using a fixed seeded item ordering independent of score and target status. Match recent/older deletions within exact accessible positions, with equal edit counts, resulting lengths, endpoint status, and contiguous/scattered patterns. Record distance changes, newly reachable content, and newly joined transitions. Use training-only transition support, frequency, timestamp gaps, and title/description similarity as coherence proxies, never inferred preferences. Keep endpoint-only replacements, endpoint-fixed shuffles, and position-preserving real-item replacements distinct. Flag implausible replacements or internal-padding interventions as stress tests. Average prefix outcomes within each user, then users within category, then the four category means equally. Missing category estimates make the macro estimate unavailable; do not silently reweight. Use 10,000 seed-42 stratified user-cluster bootstrap replicates, drawing each category's original eligible-user count with replacement and retaining all paired prefixes and candidates. Report percentile 95% intervals and state that they exclude training-seed and candidate-sampling uncertainty. Preserve existing embedding-alignment and margin probes with explicit valid counts and scale limitations; unavailable category breakdowns remain empty with reasons. Empty numeric measurements return null, never healthy-looking zeros. Run scoring without gradients in evaluation mode and restore the original mode afterward. Do not modify deployed scores, fit auxiliary scorers, or treat training diagnostics as validation improvements. If architecture or inference changes later, update the checkpoint schema, predictor writer, and adapter together. Keep preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches per epoch, all four routes, single-GPU execution, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and the host evaluator unchanged.",
    "pseudocode": "class DiagnosisProbe:\n    def __init__(self, view, device):\n        self.view, self.device = view, device\n\n    def run_full_diagnosis(self, train_loader):\n        original_mode = self.view.model.training\n        try:\n            self.view.model.eval()\n            with no_grad():\n                controls = check_readout_dependencies_normalization_and_export_parity(self.view)\n                existing = bounded_embedding_and_margin_probes(train_loader)\n                # Identity-indexed public training prefixes are required for user aggregation.\n                index = predeclared_training_prefix_index(self.view.user_train)\n                records, exclusions = [], []\n                for category, user, prefix_id, history, target in index:\n                    # history contains only events strictly before the target event.\n                    if not history:\n                        exclusions.append('empty_history')\n                        continue\n                    candidates = frozen_candidates(\n                        target=target, negative_count=99,\n                        exclude={0, target} | set(history),\n                        seed=stable_seed(42, category, user, prefix_id))\n                    if candidates is None:\n                        exclusions.append('insufficient_unique_negatives')\n                        continue\n                    variants, edit_manifest = matched_history_edits(\n                        history, exact_dependencies=controls.dependencies,\n                        fixed_edit_count=manifest.edit_count,\n                        preserve_endpoint_for_deletion_comparison=True)\n                    for condition, edited_history in variants.items():\n                        x = left_pad_and_truncate_like_predictor(edited_history)\n                        positions = nonzero_positions(x)\n                        if positions.is_empty():\n                            exclusions.append('empty_edited_history')\n                            continue\n                        j = positions.max()  # never nonzero_count - 1\n                        features = self.view.log2feats(convert_like_host(x))\n                        # Use the host-supported scoring shape, then select position j.\n                        scores = score_candidates_at_position(self.view, features, j, candidates)\n                        outcome = target_rank_margin_hit(\n                            scores, candidates, target,\n                            tie_order=frozen_target_independent_item_order)\n                        records.append((category, user, prefix_id, condition, outcome))\n                paired = pair_with_unchanged_condition(records)\n                summaries = average_prefixes_then_users_then_four_categories(paired)\n                intervals = stratified_paired_user_bootstrap(\n                    paired, replicates=10000, seed=42, missing_value=None)\n                return {\n                    'metrics': combine(existing, controls, summaries, intervals,\n                                       exclusions, empty_value=None),\n                    'metric_definitions': definitions_with_denominators_limits_and_manifest()\n                }\n        finally:\n            self.view.model.train(original_mode)\n\n# Synthetic deterministic-training controls use disposable model copies.\n# diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis) serializes measured metrics,\n# definitions, counts, missingness, and limitations. It explicitly distinguishes\n# implementation failures, in-sample sensitivity, and untested preference hypotheses.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Integrates NextItNet dependency checks, exported-predictor parity, and controlled temporal probes into one reproducible diagnostic contract.",
      "negative": "The individual techniques are established; the contribution is an application-specific integration rather than a new learning algorithm."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Creates a reusable basis for selecting subsequent single-model experiments and detecting implementation shortcuts before spending training budget.",
      "negative": "Sensitivity measurements do not establish user intent, causal preference effects, or an improvement in validation HR@10."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Most measurements reuse existing inference interfaces and require no additional trained model.",
      "negative": "Exact dependency analysis, stable prefix identities, exported scoring parity, matched interventions, and clustered uncertainty require substantial integration work."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "NextItNet",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05163",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides the residual dilated causal convolution architecture required by the model-family constraint.",
        "Supports architecture-derived checks of causal dependence and accessible history."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Architectural reachability does not establish measured influence or user intent.",
        "Exact dependency sets and normalization behavior must be derived from the supplied implementation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains why sampled ranking metrics can disagree with full-catalog model comparisons.",
        "Motivates labeling fixed-candidate diagnostic results as candidate-conditional."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the pinned evaluator.",
        "Full-catalog logged-data rankings are not automatically unbiased measurements of preference."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Measuring Recency Bias",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates measuring recency dependence rather than assuming stronger recent-item influence is beneficial."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish optimal temporal dependence for these datasets or checkpoints.",
        "A deletion-induced performance drop alone cannot identify excessive recency dependence."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Missing-input robustness study",
      "link": "https://iris.uniroma1.it/bitstream/11573/1740238/1/Siciliano_Robust-Training_2024.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents sensitivity to missing recent interactions.",
        "Motivates distinguishing missing-input robustness from interpretations of recency bias."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Endpoint-preserving shuffles, matched replacements, and the proposed intervention taxonomy are diagnostic design choices rather than established findings for this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "CETD",
      "link": "https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/13/20/11176",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates minimal temporal counterfactual edits that preserve behavioral dependencies."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Transition support and textual similarity are coherence proxies rather than observed preferences.",
        "The proposed matching procedure requires independent implementation and validation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cluster-bootstrap study",
      "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5965657/",
      "contributions": [
        "Supports resampling whole clusters to preserve within-cluster dependence.",
        "Motivates paired user-level resampling when multiple prefixes belong to one user."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The four-category stratification and macro aggregation are task-specific adaptations.",
        "Intervals conditional on fixed checkpoints and candidates exclude training and candidate-sampling uncertainty."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "NextItNet reference implementation",
      "link": "https://recommenders-team.github.io/recommenders/_modules/recommenders/models/deeprec/models/sequential/nextitnet.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides a concrete reference for causal convolution padding, sequence readout, and channel-only normalization.",
        "Supports implementation-level tests using short left-padded histories."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The host baseline may differ from this implementation.",
        "Feature-level correctness does not establish exported predictor parity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch BatchNorm1d documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.modules.batchnorm.BatchNorm1d.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Documents normalization statistics over batch and temporal dimensions for temporal inputs.",
        "Explains why normalization configuration must be included in causal-isolation audits."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Documentation describes operator behavior rather than the supplied architecture.",
        "Other normalization layers require separate axis-specific inspection."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
